# Migrating `EventProcessor` to .NET 8

## Key Problems with the Original Code

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** — removed in .NET 5+
2. **`Thread.Interrupt()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** — removed in .NET 6+
3. **Race condition**: multiple enqueues with one `WaitOne`/`Reset` cycle loses items
4. **No gracellation support** — no clean shutdown path

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class EventProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private Task? _processingTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _processingTask = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken ct)
    {
        try
        {
            await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(ct))
            {
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            // Expected on shutdown
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        if (!_channel.Writer.TryWrite(item))
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Failed to write to channel.");
    }

    public async ValueTask StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete();

        if (_cts is not null)
        {
            // Give processing a chance to drain, then force-cancel
            var effectiveTimeout = timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
            using var timeoutCts = new CancellationTokenSource(effectiveTimeout);

            try
            {
                await (_processingTask ?? Task.CompletedTask)
                    .WaitAsync(timeoutCts.Token);
            }
            catch (TimeoutException)
            {
                // Drain didn't finish in time — cancel the loop
                await _cts.CancelAsync();
                await (_processingTask ?? Task.CompletedTask)
                    .ConfigureAwait(ConfigureAwaitOptions.SuppressThrowing);
            }
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await StopAsync();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item)
    {
        // Your processing logic here
    }
}
```

## Migration Pitfalls

| Issue | .NET Framework | .NET 8 |
|-------|---------------|--------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `ThreadAbortException` | **`PlatformNotSupportedException`** |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Wakes blocking calls | **`PlatformNotSupportedException`** |
| Cancellation | Ad-hoc signals | Use `CancellationToken` everywhere |
| Queue + lock + event | Manual synchronization | `Channel<T>` (lock-free, backpressure-aware) |
| Lost items | Race between `Set`/`Reset` | Channels handle this inherently |

## Why `Channel<T>`?

- **Thread-safe** without explicit locks
- **Backpressure** via bounded channels if needed (`CreateBounded<T>(capacity)`)
- **Async-native** — no thread blocked while waiting
- **Completion semantics** — `Writer.Complete()` signals graceful drain

## If You Need Synchronous Semantics

Use `BlockingCollection<T>` as a simpler drop-in:

```csharp
private readonly BlockingCollection<string> _queue = new();

// Producer
_queue.Add(item);

// Consumer loop
foreach (var item in _queue.GetConsumingEnumerable(ct))
    Handle(item);

// Stop
_queue.CompleteAdding();
```

This is closer to the original pattern but still avoids `Abort`/`Interrupt`.
